Dining Hall (五觀堂 / 齋堂)
Lian Shan Shuang Lin Monastery

Preliminary observation
The hall presents an unusually rich case of doctrinal naming layered over functional space. Two names appear simultaneously, encoding two distinct registers of the space's identity:
五觀堂  (Wǔ Guān Táng)
  -- "Hall of Five Contemplations"
  -- the doctrinally significant name
  -- the upper plaque

齋堂 / DINING HALL  (Zhāi Táng)
  -- "Vegetarian Dining Hall" / "Refectory"
  -- the functional name
  -- the foreground signage
The two names operate at different scopes. 齋堂 identifies what the room is used for — communal vegetarian dining for the monastic community and visitors. 五觀堂 identifies what should occur in the mind of the person eating. The former is operational; the latter is theological.
The Five Contemplations (五觀) refer to a canonical set of contemplative reflections that Chinese Buddhist monastics recite or hold in mind before each meal, drawn from the Vinaya tradition:
1. 計功多少，量彼來處
   Reflect on the labour involved in producing this food
2. 忖己德行，全缺應供
   Consider whether one's virtue is sufficient to merit the offering
3. 防心離過，貪等為宗
   Guard the mind against greed, anger, and other faults
4. 正事良藥，為療形枯
   Receive the food as medicine for the body's deficiency
5. 為成道業，應受此食
   Eat only to accomplish the Way
These five contemplations transform eating from a mundane biological activity into a soteriological discipline. By naming the dining hall after them, the institution encodes the contemplative practice directly into the architectural identity of the space. To enter 五觀堂 is to enter a space whose name reminds the diner that monastic eating is itself a form of cultivation.

Step 1 — ICON description
ICON₁ — Budai (布袋, Cloth-Sack Maitreya) (Central altar of the dining hall)
POSITION    : central, axial, recessed (on altar shelf)
SCALE       : singular
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : devotional, pedagogical
ICONOGRAPHIC REGISTER : Chinese Mahāyāna folk-Buddhist
                        (jade/celadon ceramic in seated form)
The principal figure is a small jade-coloured Budai — the laughing, large-bellied, exposed-bellied form of Maitreya — seated on the altar in his canonical posture of joyful openness. The choice of Budai for a dining hall is iconographically precise:
- Budai is iconographically associated with food
  (he carries a cloth sack 布袋 from which he distributes
   gifts and accepts offerings)

- He represents abundance, contentment, and unconditional
  welcome — appropriate qualities for a space of communal
  nourishment

- His exposed belly and broad smile encode generosity
  and the receiving of offerings without discrimination

- As Maitreya in his folk-iconographic form, he also encodes
  the future Buddha's accessibility to all sentient beings
The contrast with the Maitreya at the Hall of Heavenly Kings is worth noting. There, Maitreya appears in his welcoming-threshold function — large, prominent, the figure who admits the devotee into the monastery. 
Here, in the dining hall, Budai appears in his abundance-and-receiving function — smaller in scale, presiding intimately over a space of nourishment, encoding the spirit of joyful provision and acceptance that should characterize the meal taken in his presence.
